// devices/regex: patterns as JavaScript writes them, matched by a machine
// that follows every way at once. Each line of Inputs/regex/cases.txt (the
// pattern, its flags, a text) and where the first match starts and ends,
// as JavaScript says (node's RegExp exec, in bytes of UTF-8), or why the
// pattern is none: an ISO time, an email, flags i m s, alternatives and
// lazy repeats in JavaScript's order, classes with \d \w \s and their
// opposites, \b, UTF-8, escapes, [] and [^], what needs going back (\1,
// looking ahead or behind) said to be unsupported, wrong patterns, and
// (a*)*b and (a|aa)+$ that hang a backtracking matcher.
// RUN: env REGEX_CASES=%S/Inputs/regex/cases.txt %ent run %S/Inputs/regex/cases.ent 2>/dev/null > %t.out
// RUN: FileCheck %s --input-file=%t.out --match-full-lines

